---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('sequenzy')!;
const b = appBySlug('shopify-email')!;
const path = '/compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Sequenzy or Shopify Email for cart recovery?', answer: 'Start by auditing the native Shopify recovery automation already available to your store. Evaluate Sequenzy when its store-data segmentation and email/SMS lifecycle workflows solve a specific operating requirement. Demonstrate that requirement before moving the recovery queue.' },
  { question: 'Does Shopify have abandoned checkout automation?', answer: 'Yes. Current Shopify documentation places recovery automations in Shopify Messaging. Inspect the installed automation and recipient settings rather than assuming Shopify Email only sends newsletters.' },
  { question: 'Can both systems send recovery reminders?', answer: 'An active connection does not establish coordination. Assign one owner per recovery event, inspect pending messages in both systems, and demonstrate purchase and unsubscribe handling before enabling a replacement.' },
  { question: 'How should a discount affect the comparison?', answer: 'For a hypothetical $78 basket at 46% product gross margin, unchanged product cost is $42.12. A 10% discount leaves $70.20 revenue and $28.08 product gross profit, before shipping, payment and sending costs. It reduces profit by $7.80, not $3.59.' },
  { question: 'What proves a migration is ready?', answer: 'Use designated profiles to trace eligibility, queued messages, checkout return links, purchases and withdrawals. A layout test email alone does not prove event enrollment or that the previous sender has stopped.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Do you need a separate recovery platform, or can your store's native automation do the job? Compare the actual recovery queue, customer eligibility and maintenance work before adding another sender.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Native starting point', tool: b.name, reason: 'Audit Shopify Messaging recovery before replacing it.' }, { label: 'Lifecycle evaluation', tool: a.name, reason: 'Evaluate store-data segments and email/SMS journeys against an unmet requirement.' }]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Begin with the automation you already have</h2>
        <p>The route uses the Shopify Email name; current Shopify help places marketing and recovery automations in Shopify Messaging. Its <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">automation guide</a> describes abandoned checkout workflows and editable recipient settings. Native recovery is a real baseline, not a missing feature that automatically requires Sequenzy.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Sequenzy Shopify listing</a> describes store-data synchronization, purchase-based segments and email/SMS lifecycle flows. Those capabilities make it a candidate when a team needs to connect recovery with later customer journeys. Published scope, checked November 24, 2026, does not prove a particular account's event latency or suppression behavior.</p>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Native Shopify baseline</th><th>Sequenzy evaluation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Recovery ownership</td><td>Inspect the active checkout workflow, recipients and pending sends in Shopify.</td><td>Identify exactly which native workflow it would replace and how existing queued reminders will be handled.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Customer context</td><td>Try the conditions needed for your actual recovery policy.</td><td>Demonstrate the required purchase-history or product segment with synchronized store data.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operator work</td><td>Measure the effort to maintain recovery within the Shopify admin.</td><td>Compare the work to manage recovery alongside welcome, post-purchase and win-back journeys.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cost basis</td><td>Separate checkout recovery email treatment from other marketing sends and paid messaging channels.</td><td>Quote the needed email volume, SMS destinations and plan features, including migration effort.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Proposed trial: replace a native queue without doubling it</h2>
        <p>This is an acceptance exercise, not an observed merchant result. Use designated profiles and record the current native automation's entry conditions, recipient setting, delay and pending messages. Shopify's <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">migration guide</a> distinguishes the recovery automation from the older checkout setup; identify which version your store actually uses.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Establish the baseline:</strong> create an eligible online checkout without completing payment. Record whether it enters the native queue and why. Inspect the resulting checkout link and basket content separately from the email layout.</li>
          <li><strong>Test the proposed replacement:</strong> demonstrate the equivalent checkout event and identity in Sequenzy. Compare timestamps and eligibility decisions; an imported address does not establish a recoverable checkout.</li>
          <li><strong>Complete the purchase while waiting:</strong> inspect both queues and their actual send decisions. Define how pending native reminders will be stopped during cutover rather than assuming one platform cancels the other.</li>
          <li><strong>Withdraw permission or change eligibility:</strong> inspect what each active workflow does before its next message. Repeat an event delivery to check that a retry does not enroll the same checkout twice.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Keep an auditable record of which sender owns each event at each cutover step. For rollback, record which workflow will resume, which pending messages have been canceled and how you will avoid replaying already contacted checkouts. Do not run competing live default journeys just to compare dashboards.</p>
        <h2>Decide whether the extra customer context changes an action</h2>
        <p>A useful Sequenzy demonstration would distinguish a first-order checkout from a returning customer's checkout using the store's purchase history, then show the specific message or exclusion that changes. For example, a proposed returning-buyer branch could offer help with a product compatibility question instead of repeating a new-customer signup offer. Verify segment membership and the actual message before adopting that policy; this is a design proposal, not a claim about automatic behavior.</p>
        <p>Try the same requirement with the native workflow. If both configurations deliver the intended experience, compare maintenance effort and the cost of keeping another profile store synchronized. If neither can identify why the shopper stopped, adding an incentive or another channel does not supply the missing context.</p>
        <h2>Use product economics rather than an arbitrary incentive cap</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $78 basket at 46% product gross margin has $35.88 full-price product gross profit and $42.12 product cost. A 10% code removes $7.80 of revenue. Discounted revenue is $70.20; at unchanged cost, product gross profit is $28.08. Shipping, payment fees, returns and message costs reduce contribution further. Neither sender makes that discount cheaper.</p>
        <p>Compare a helpful reminder with an incentive branch only when the offer is eligible and affordable. Preserve existing code restrictions and test an already discounted basket. Report discount spend alongside orders, returns and contribution. If traffic supports a controlled comparison, size it for the decision and buying cycle; a universal holdout percentage or platform-attributed revenue total cannot establish incremental profit.</p>
        <h2>When to retain native recovery and when to evaluate Sequenzy</h2>
        <p>Retain native recovery when it satisfies the required eligibility, content and operating workflow, and another system would mainly duplicate it. Evaluate Sequenzy when a demonstrated segment or lifecycle requirement justifies the additional setup and gives the team a clearer place to maintain customer journeys. Neither choice is an automatic performance winner.</p>
        <p>Shopify's automation documentation excludes abandoned checkout emails from its email-send count while distinguishing charges for messaging channels. Use that actual billing treatment in the comparison. Obtain the Sequenzy quote for the proposed usage and include synchronization, support and ongoing workflow maintenance. A starting monthly price alone is not the total cost of replacing native recovery.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
